Now, we will create Entity class. When we create Entity class, our subpackage name should be part of base package. Otherwise, Spring Boot won't be able to scan the package. Since base package name is com.example.hibernate, all our further packages should be sub-package of this package. So, we will name the package for entity class as com.example.hibernate.entity. Some beginners might create this package in src/test/java instead of src/main/java which will cause issues and it won't work as per our requirement. All our Java backend code goes to src/main/java and all our test code goes to src/test/java. Create Employee entity class in com.example.hibernate.entity package. Its mandatory for an Entity class to have primary key else it will throw error.

This entity class will automatically generate employees table with mentioned column names and constraints in database.

Encapsulation is mandatory as it hides internal structure of object. If we don't do encapsulation, we will compromise with safety. When data needs to be persisted, the object is serialized into 0’s and 1’s. That serialized object travels over the network or to the persistence layer, where it is deserialized back into an object. The ORM or database layer then extracts the object’s fields and stores the content (field values) in the database server, typically as rows and columns rather than the raw serialized object.
Object → serialized → transported → deserialized → mapped → stored in DB.

package com.example.hibernate.entity;

//All imports will be done from jakarta.persistence.* package.
import jakarta.persistence.Column;
import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.GenerationType;
import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;

@Entity
//Database table name is plural form unlike Entity class name. When entity class and database table name is different, we use annotation, @Table(name = "employees"). This means that entity class Employee is mapped to our database table name, employee.
@Table(name = "employees")
public class Employee {
 
// To mark id as primary key, we use @Id annotation
 @Id
// We want id to be auto-generated like we use auto-increment in MySQL. For this we use annotation, @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
 @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
 private long id;
 
// We want the name column to be not null. So, we will use annotation, @Column(name = "name", length = 45, nullable = false) for this constraint
 @Column(name = "name", length = 45, nullable = false)
 private String name;
 
// Since emailId is in camel case and in database it will be in snake case, so we will use annotation, @Column(name = "email_id") else it will throw error.
 @Column(name = "email_id", length = 45, nullable = false, unique = true)
 private String emailId;
 
@Column(name = "mobile_no", length = 10, nullable = false, unique = true)
 private String mobileNo;

}

