To perform CRUD operation, we have to write lots of code in traditional approach and we use a concept known as session factory, open session. Session Factory gives us certain methods using which we can perform CRUD operation. In order to simplify this, they introduced a concept known as Repository Layer which means if we want to perform any database operations on our entity class, project should have repository layer present.

Entity class, Employee need to have Repository to perform DB operations as this Repository will be responsible to perform DB operations. Repository Layer concept comes from Spring Boot. They introduced Repository Layer to simplify implementation of Hibernate. Every Entity class needs to have their own Repository Layer means if we will create another Entity class, Student then we need to have separate repository for Student. Without Repository layer, we can't perform DB operations. One Repository Layer is responsible to do CRUD operation for one entity class.

Create one package, com.example.hibernate.repository in src/main/java. This package should be sub package of Group Id. To create Repository Layer, we will create one interface, EmployeeRepository in this package. Once we create interface, we need to make sure that it extends CrudRepository<T, ID> which is a built-in interface. Now, we need to mention Entity class and wrapper class of primary key. This is done via CrudRepository generics, e.g. CrudRepository<Employee, Long>. Repository layer will give us all the required methods to perform database operation on Entity class.
