Spring Boot understands StartingWith because Spring Data JPA has a built-in dictionary of keywords that map directly to SQL/JPQL clauses. StartingWith means “prefix match,” so it generates a LIKE 'prefix%' query automatically.
1. Method Name Parsing
When Spring Data starts, it scans repository interfaces. Each method name is broken down into:
Prefix → find, read, get, count, exists
Property → matches an entity field (Name, EmailId, etc.)
Keyword → special operators like StartingWith, EndingWith, Containing, Between, GreaterThan, etc.
2. Keyword Dictionary
Spring Data JPA maintains a predefined set of keywords. Each keyword maps to a specific JPQL/SQL fragment:
StartingWith → LIKE 'prefix%'
EndingWith → LIKE '%suffix'
Containing → LIKE '%substring%'
GreaterThan → > value
Between → BETWEEN value1 AND value2
IsNull → IS NULL
True / False → boolean checks
3. Why It’s Reliable
The dictionary is hardcoded in Spring Data JPA — we can’t invent new keywords.
If we mistype (StartWith instead of StartingWith), Spring won’t recognize it and will throw an error.
This makes query derivation predictable and consistent.

