####################################################
JPQL (Java Persistence Query Language) in Hibernate
#####################################################

JPQL stands for Java Persistence Query Language. It is similar to SQL, but:
-> It works with Java entity objects, not directly with database tables.
-> It uses entity names and field/property names, not table or column names.

****************************************************
What is the difference b/w JPQL and MySQL?
SQL queries are written down on DB tables not on entity classes. While in JPQl, we write queries for entity class.

Whenever we are writing JPQL, we need to understand what that method is going to return. It will generally return Optional object or Entity object. We can give any name to the methods. It doesn't need to follow a certain template like in finder method. We need to annotate that method with @Query() and in @Query() , we need to write the query as shown below.

@Query("select e from Employee e where e.emailId=:x")
Employee searchByEmail(@Param("x") String email)

Since entity class name starts with uppercase, it needs to start with uppercase in query as well else it won't work.
For entity class, we have to give a shortcut name like e, that is rule over here.
In the o/p, we want all the fields that belongs to this partciular entity class. That's why, we use e from.
Email Id provided by us should be substituted dynamically in query. For this, we use annotation, @Param with any name like ("x"). This means wherever in my query, we will use x, it will be replaced with email provided by us.
We can consider e as an object of Entity class or reference to this entity, Employee. This means whatever variable is present in class, those can be accessed using the object, e.
 
